.TH DICTATE 1
.SH NAME
dictate \- type what is said
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dictate
[
.B -w
] [
.B -m
.I speechdir
] [
.B -o
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dictate
turns
.IR speech (4)'s
.B talk
on (with
.BR -w ,
waits for it to be turned on), waits for it to be turned off, and types
the words of that utterance's final into
.I file
.RB ( /dev/keyboard ,
so they go wherever the keyboard types:
.IR cons (3)),
followed by a space.
Talk is turned off by whoever turns it off: a key, a tap, push to talk.
.I Speechdir
is
.B /mnt/speech
unless given.
.PP
It hears one utterance, the one it began (or, with
.BR -w ,
the next to begin), and refuses if talk is on already.
An error from the engine is printed and nothing is typed.
.SS Lucifer
Lucifer's header shows the microphone, at its right, while there is
speech: dim while talk is off, red while it is on, and lit in the
theme's accent while an agent waits for an answer (speech's
.BR ask ).
It is yellow while it listens for the wake phrase, if the user has set
one
.RI ( speech (4));
a command said after it is the current activity's next message, as
if typed and sent.
Ctrl+Space, or a tap on the microphone, switches it: off if it is on;
on to answer the agent, if one asks (the words are the agent's, not
typed); otherwise on for
.IR dictate ,
which types the words where the keyboard types once it is switched
off.
A chime marks each.
.SH EXAMPLE
Dictate from a shell; turn talk off from elsewhere when done:
.IP
.EX
dictate &
echo stop > /mnt/speech/talk
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/dictate.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR speech (4),
.IR cons (3)
